  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Pivetta gave them clean innings and they never blew the lead, but the real test was the eighth inning where the bats had to finish what the pitching built.",
    "what_this_means": "They needed to hold what they had. Pivetta delivered five scoreless, surrendering just two hits, and the bullpen moved through four scoreless frames after. That structure. A starter who doesn't break and relievers who don't collapse. Kept the door open. Jackson Merrill and Fernando Tatis Jr. both went 3-for-4, and Merrill's two RBIs in the third inning gave them enough to work with. But the eighth inning exposed what happens when they can't convert. Three runners on base, nothing scored. They had the game. They couldn't put it away until Mason Miller closed it out. This is a team that survives on thin margins because the pitching doesn't give away outs. But that only works if the bats don't waste the chances they get. Two runs in nine innings is not resilience. It's survival gear.",
    "what_to_watch": "Riley Cornelio starts for Washington tonight with a 5.96 ERA, and Casey Mize takes the mound for the Padres looking to extend what has become a fragile two-game streak. One clean inning from Mize beats Cornelio's volatility every time.",
